onomic numbers for  2021 and so far so good the economy grew  0.7  in january that is the ninth monthly  increase in a row since the gdp took a  plunge  last spring the growth is better than  expected though stats canada says  economic activity is still running about  three percent below  pre-pandemic levels the positive start  to the year  is boosting consumer confidence at least  for those who have  jobs economists at the conference board  of canada are predicting  big things for 2021 and as ann gaviola  explains we could be on the cusp of a  roaring twenties revival  the roaring 20s swept across america  after the deadly 1918 flu pandemic  the similarities between then and now  are striking  technological advances were drastically  changing people's lives  the stock market was soaring and people  were ready to cut loose and spend money  i'm going to try and say yes more  brianna davies has big plans for when  things get back to some semblance of  normal  having so many of the things we like  take for granted  every day gone like the ability to like  go grab a glass of wine with a  with a girlfriend at a new little spot  canadians have been saving like never  before  collectively we've stockpiled more than  200 billion dollars worth  now a lot of that has found its way into  the stock market been used to pay down  debt or to renovate and buy homes  but economists estimate there's more  than 100 billion dollars worth just  waiting to be spent  a big pickup starting in july and  carrying until early 2022.  but supply chain experts say headaches  are in store a flurry of shoppers may  mean shortages  remember what happened with toilet paper  at the onset of the pandemic  but economists warn that if shoppers are  spooked by higher prices  they may rush out to buy things before  prices skyrocket  creating shortages and stoking inflation  so there's a sort of  self-fulfilling prophecy that could  occur  if there's an increase in inflation  expectations  this roaring 20s style comeback is  dependent on some moving targets the  pace of the vaccine rollout  the consequences of the more contagious  coronavirus variants  the impact of new financial aid from  ottawa as well as the time it takes for  the ripple effect of the nearly 2  trillion in stimulus from the us  to jolt canada's economy briana's family  is dreaming of the day they can travel  again their bucket list safari vacation  to south africa was cut short by the  pandemic last march  i really rely on travel as a bit of an  escape  to you know hang out with my family the  feeling of looking forward to a trip  and not just the actual trip but  planning the trip and looking forward to  it  gets me through a lot of like stressful  long days  and gaviola global news toronto  u.s president joe biden is ready to  spend money and a lot of it  today he unveiled his plan to help  america build back better  what i'm proposing is a one-time capital  investment of roughly two trillion  dollars in america's  future spread largely over eight years  will generate historic job growth  historic economic growth  help businesses to compete  internationally create more revenue as  well  biden hopes to create millions of jobs  by investing in infrastructure across  the country the projects would be  financed in part by an increase in  corporate taxes  the plan still needs to be passed by  congress  next did an automaker's prank backfire  we've all heard the saying there's no  such thing as bad publicity well  volkswagen definitely got lots of  publicity this week but  lots of people are talking about it for  all the wrong reasons the german company  says what happened this week was an  early april fool's joke and as mike  armstrong explains  not everyone is laughing  [Music]  now there's nothing strange about what  volkswagen was trying to do  another reporter might have said nothing  shocking it's the same thing it's set  out to accomplish with this recent act  i think we're done yet to get the public  to associate vw  with electric vehicles on our way to  carbon neutral mobility for all now it's  one thing to do this  it's another to do what the company did  tuesday  it pretended to accidentally leak a name  change with this twitter video  becoming volt's wagon that's volts as in  electricity  well if journalists were fooled and many  were there were  reasons volkswagen issued a press  release quoting its ceo  and when reporters called to confirm it  all they were reassured  it was true all evs in the u.s will have  volkswagen badging on the rear  wagon the news went around the world on  tv  and online from the usa today to  publications back in volkswagen's home  country germany  well tuesday night a retraction the  company issued a statement explaining it  was all in the spirit of  april fool's day some of the journalists  who covered the story didn't end up  laughing usa today's nathan bowmy  tweeted out a message  dear volkswagen you lied to me you lied  to ap  cnbc reuters and various trade  publications  this was not a joke it was deception  it's wrong it's just simply wrong and  marketing expert robert soroca says vw  broke some rules of advertising but may  have also broken some laws  volkswagen's stock jumped about five  percent when the prank news broke  this is a manipulation of the market and  it's done in a way that's  it's unfair it's it's done on the  premise of a lie  immobility health this supposed prank  comes with volkswagen  still trying to rebuild its brand after  the 2015 emissions scandal the company  had to pay out 45 billion dollars in  fines and settlements  one marketing expert says that may be  the biggest problem with the prank  it's related to the environment the same  issue and got into trouble for  the last time mike armstrong global news  and that is global national for this  wednesday i'm donna friesen  this is international transgender day of  visibility and there are plenty of  canadians to celebrate  here are just a few listed by one of  canada's largest unions  in toronto there's raven wings a black  indigenous artist and activist  monica forrester has spent two decades  fighting for the rights of trans people  and sex workers  manitoba's kale mckenzie is canada's  first transgender judge  and alberta's vivek shreya and yukon's  ivan coyote  are award-winning authors musicians  filmmakers and educators  thanks for watching hope to see you here  again tomorrow take care of yourselves  bye-bye  [Music]  you
 

